WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.919
Hello! How do you feel when you find a
vulnerability

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:07.839
with cool filter bypass? Because I feel
absolutely amazing!

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:12.719
But today i have for you a vulnerability
where four filters were bypassed

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:17.680
to exploit XSS on airbnb website.
The vulnerability was found by Brett

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.720
Bauerhaus and Ben Sadeghipour
aka NahamSec. He also has a youtube

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.279
channel
full of talks with great bug bounty

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:28.400
hunters.
And my today's video is based on a

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.880
write-up from Brett's blog.
The link as always is in the description.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.440
enjoy!

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.760
so on embeddable/listing_frame endpoint
on airbnb, data from parameter

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.320
city-link-index was reflected inside
the JSON body and the first thing

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.000
that you need to know is that there is
script with type

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.600
application/json and inside such tag
no javascript code gets executed.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.199
But if you can smuggle a closing script
tag,

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:07.520
even inside the string in the json, the
tag gets closed.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.439
And then you can open another script tag,
this time the normal one and execute

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:18.240
javascript code inside.
But of course it didn't work in this

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.479
case, because if it would
I wouldn't make a video about it.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:26.640
Let's start
with bypassing xss filter

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.439
so if you send any HTML tag in the value
of the parameter

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:37.040
it got deleted, but if you would put
a semicolon at the beginning of the

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.920
parameter value,
the tag would not be deleted. And one

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.920
thing to notice
is that previously the payload was in

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.399
the JSON value,
but the semicolon made the parser think

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:54.240
that this value is ended and another key-value pair

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.840
is created and likely this is what
confused

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:05.040
the xss filter. And you might be thinking
why are they using a &lt;u&gt; tag instead of a

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.200
&lt;script&gt; tag.
The reason is that if you have

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.440
multiple filters present,
you want to separate them and bypass

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.400
them one by one.
So first you need to see if you can

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.319
inject
any HTML code and for that purpose

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:29.680
the least dangerous tags are the best.
So we have bypassed one protection and

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.200
we can
close the &lt;script&gt; tag and now it's time

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.800
to open another tag
with our javascript code. But this time

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.879
web application firewall prevents us.
Now what's the difference between xss

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:48.800
filter
and web application firewall? The former

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.560
works inside the application code and
it's only used

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.879
in some specific places. Whereas web
application firewall

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:00.319
or WAF is more like a plugin to the
server

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:06.319
which analyzes all the requests.
And it was rather secure because it

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:12.080
prevented the researchers
from using an opening script tag.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:18.239
The only way it was possible to smuggle
the script tag, was using url encoded

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:26.400
null byte inside it. It got converted,
but it was not a valid HTML script tag.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.640
But luckily javascript code can also be
executed

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.159
in other places, for example in
attributes

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:40.319
of another tags. Example of that
is image tag with onerror attribute

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:45.840
that gets triggered.
WAF also blocked function calls, but

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.760
luckily
this could be bypassed by inserting

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.240
a comment between the function name
and the parenthesis.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.680
So now, it was time to try different HTML
attributes

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.720
to execute code. And up to this point
the payload was always in JSON key,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.560
but when an equal sign was sent, then
everything

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.120
before it was treated as the JSON key
and everything after

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.919
was treated as the value. So the first
attribute would always be ignored by the

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:25.759
browser,
because there would be no equal sign but

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:29.600
using forward slashes as attribute
separators,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:37.120
it was possible to inject more than one
attribute. But of course the attributes

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.680
were also blocked by the firewall. And
the same bypass

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.040
that did not work with the script tag,
now

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.600
worked with the attributes because the
null bytes were getting discarded

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.479
leaving valid attribute names.
So finally we have a way to smuggle

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:00.400
arbitrary attributes so we can send a
classic

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.880
image payload and it should work.
But it didn't as there was also content

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:14.080
security policy.
What is it? CSP limits what resources you

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.639
can use on the website.
It's additional layer of security that

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:21.120
helps
to mitigate cross-site scripting

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.440
vulnerabilities.
You can define domains that you trust

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.800
and you only want to execute scripts
from that site.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:32.240
You can also tell the browser that you
do not trust

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.880
any inline scripts on the website and
you do not want browser to

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.360
execute them. And on airbnb
the CSP part that's interesting to us

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:50.479
looked like this. And the sha-256
part tells the browser that it can only

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:54.080
execute
scripts that have checksum equal to this

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.720
value.
And this includes both standard script

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.600
tags,
as well as javascript in the attributes.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.720
And without getting deep into
cryptography

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.759
we can say that it's impossible to
create

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:17.280
a script that would have a valid checksum.
And if a script element could be used

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.639
it would be possible to find some
bypasses to the content security policy,

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:25.759
but previous attempts have already shown
this

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:32.560
to be a really tall order.
So the xss has to be exploited using

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.880
another tag. And at the time of recording
this video,

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.520
in 2020, flash is being dropped by modern
browsers,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:47.039
but back in 2017, when this report was
initially submitted, flash could be

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:52.319
widely used
to exploit XSS or JSON CSRFs.

00:06:52.319 --> 00:06:59.199
And using an embed tag it's possible
to use swf file to execute

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:04.800
an actionscript file. This is the code
that was used by the hackers.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.160
i won't go into the detail all that's
important

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.800
is that this line executes javascript
code.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:19.199
And finally the alert popped up,
but the web application firewall

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:22.240
happened out
to be really annoying and the same

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.240
payload
sometimes worked and sometimes didn't.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.759
And this is the payload that would work
every single time.

00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:35.919
And at this point you might be thinking
what was the process

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:40.080
of getting from one payload to another.
And the truth is

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.000
in such case the process is simply trial
and error.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.479
You just have to submit many many
payloads and suddenly

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:52.800
one of them works, because in such
complicated case,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.599
when the same payload one time works and
one time doesn't,

00:07:57.599 --> 00:08:01.440
it's impossible to reverse engineer and
understand

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.120
what actually gets filtered and what
doesn't.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.879
And this payload only worked in firefox
as in google chrome there was xss

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:15.199
auditor,
which is also dropped in 2020.

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:21.199
And after another series of payloads
sent to the server this is what worked

00:08:21.199 --> 00:08:26.960
both in chrome and firefox
every single time. And as the site relied

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.879
on those protections the researchers
managed to find

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:37.039
seven more occurrences of this xss
including store ones, which of course

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:41.839
have bigger impact.
The exact amount of bounty rewarded for

00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:45.839
this report
is not known. Back then airbnb paid five

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:49.600
thousand dollars
for simple stored xss and a little less

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.880
for reflected one.
Here, in total, we have eight

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.399
vulnerabilities,
but they have the same root cause so my

00:08:56.399 --> 00:09:00.720
guess the vulnerability was paid
around fifteen thousand dollars and

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.959
that's definitely
the best XSS I have ever seen.

00:09:04.959 --> 00:09:09.360
If you've enjoyed it as much as i did
or you've liked my explanation leave a

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:13.680
like and a subscription down below
and for now i wish you happy hunting and

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.240
goodbye!

